"PitchBook API - PitchBook (https://pitchbook.com/help/PitchBook-api)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pitchbook.com/help/PitchBook-api\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 81\nL0: Search cite0†Request a free trial cite1†Log in†my.pitchbook.com L1: \nL2: [Input: Search]\nL3: \nL4: # Help: PitchBook API\nL5: \nL6: Find information about PitchBook’s API solution and discover how it can meet your business needs. Our API is a separate offering from the PitchBook Platform that requires a standalone contract agreement.\nL7: ### Jump to\nL8: \nL9:   * cite2†Overview L10:   * cite3†Use cases L11:   * cite4†How the API works L12: \nL13: Overview\nL14: \nL15: PitchBook’s API solution allows you to call specific PitchBook data into your internal business systems on demand. This flexible solution means you have access to PitchBook’s expansive database. It’s the ideal integration for CRM enrichment, allowing you to extract data on an ad-hoc basis that meets the needs of your organization.\nL16: You can explore a specific entity and retrieve valuable data, and the API solution enables you to apply specific criteria, such as location and industry, to drill down into the businesses you discover.\nL17: ### Not a PitchBook customer?\nL18: \nL19: cite5†Request free trial L20: \nL21: Use cases\nL22: \nL23: The API is a good solution if:\nL24: \nL25:   * Your scope and data points are subject to change frequently.\nL26:   * You require a near-instant population of data.\nL27:   * You perform ad-hoc research.\nL28:   * You want to see past and present financial data.\nL29:   * RESTful API is a system requirement.\nL30: ### Ready to get started?\nL31: \nL32: cite6†Log in†my.pitchbook.com L33: \nL34: How the API works\nL35: \nL36: Accessing the API\nL37: The PitchBook API is a RESTful implementation that most HTTP clients can easily access in your chosen language or via a web browser. Every call will use the same base URL: cite7†https://api.pitchbook.com†api.pitchbook.com L38: Available data points\nL39: The API solution offers a variety of relational endpoints, including financing details, deal stock information, as well as AI-powered VC exit predictor data. See the full list from our cite8†API Endpoints Overview document.†pitchbook.highspot.com L40: \nL41: Create your API connection\nL42: To start your API connection, you will need to send a request directly to our Direct Data team cite9†through this link. L43: Or if you’re a PitchBook client, you can also head to the Plugins & Apps section from the My PitchBook section of the left-hand sidebar on the PitchBook Platform and choose PitchBook API.\nL44: \nL45: cite10†Image: Plugins and apps section of left-hand side-bar.jpg†pitchbook.brightspotcdn.com L46: \nL47: From this section on the platform, you can test specific endpoints. This section on PitchBook also allows you to reach out to our Direct Data team to request access to the API.\nL48: ##\nL49: ## Next steps\nL50: \nL51: Our team will work with you to understand the insights and data you’re looking for and help you establish the API connection through an API key or authentication token. Available data for each entity type is packaged across several endpoints so you can map the data to your desired format and structure.\nL52: \nL53: cite11†Image: 2024-globsl-g2-logo.svg†pitchbook.brightspotcdn.com L54: > ### “PitchBook is the gold standard for data on privately-backed companies and the VC and PE ecosystem. Over the years they have expanded their coverage to provide excellent data on public companies and M&A as well, and have vastly increased the coverage on international companies. The platform is intuitive and easy-to-use and customer service is top-notch.”\nL55: \nL56: —Steven Medley, Senior Market Intelligence Manager, Sidley Austin LLP\nL57: \nL58: Source : G2.com\nL59: \nL60: cite12†View our G2 reviews »†www.g2.com L61: ## Access PitchBook.\nL62: Act confidently.\nL63: \nL64: [Input]\nL65: \nL66: Email [Input: Email address]\nL67: \nL68: [Button: Request a free trial ]\nL69: \nL70: Follow Us\nL71: \nL72:   *   *   *   *   *   * cite13†Products L73:     *     *     *     *     *   * cite14†Insights L74:     *     *     *     *   *     *     *     *     * Follow Us\nL75: \nL76:   *   *   *   *   * ©2026 PitchBook. All rights reserved.\nL77: \nL78: cite15†Terms of Use cite16†Privacy Policy cite16†Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information cite17†GDPR Compliance L79: \nL80: cite18†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.datadoghq.com/account_management/api-app-keys/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://docs.datadoghq.com/account_management/api-app-keys/: (400) Unsupported content-type: text/markdown\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccount settings - Gumroad Help Center (https://gumroad.com/help/article/67-the-settings-menu)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gumroad.com/help/article/67-the-settings-menu\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLicense keys - Gumroad Help Center (https://gumroad.com/help/article/76-license-keys)\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gumroad.com/help/article/76-license-keys\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer-docs.amazon/sp-api/docs/registering-as-a-developer\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://developer-docs.amazon/sp-api/docs/registering-as-a-developer is not safe to open (non-retryable error)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nB2C Commerce Architecture | B2C Commerce | B2C Commerce API | Salesforce Developers (https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/commerce-api/guide/get-started.html)\nciteturn6view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/commerce-api/guide/get-started.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 68\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: # B2C Commerce Architecture\nL2: \nL3: We provide several resources to help you learn about Salesforce B2C Commerce.\nL4: \nL5: For a summary of the developer workflow, see cite1†Get Started with B2C Commerce .\nL6: \nL7: B2C Commerce provides the resources and processing for your ecommerce sites, called a storefront in the B2C Commerce documentation. You create and build your sites in your realm.\nL8: \nL9: A customer realm includes a primary instance group and a secondary instance group.\nL10: Typically, you have a single realm. The realm contains instances on which to develop, test, and deploy your online storefront. A B2C Commerce instance is an application infrastructure. It includes web servers, application servers, and database servers. You usually have six B2C Commerce instances: three or more sandbox instances for code development, and three instances for site staging, testing, and deployment. Development instance: used for site configuration, data enrichment, and data import.\nL11: Staging instance: used to test your site before deployment. Production instance: hosts the live site that is used by customers. A Merchant can control their storefront online merchandising, configuration, and customization options. The instance administrator for the merchant has direct control over password management, user permissions, and stopping or starting instances within their realm.\nL12: In most cases, you have a single realm in which to develop, stage, and deploy multiple sites with different branding or locales. The people managing the storefront site don’t have to be in the same location. Sites can share product catalogs or have different catalogs.\nL13: If you have different lines of business or global teams that each have their own processes or business policies, consider maintaining multiple realms. Also consider separate realms if you have distinct organizations with separate back-end integrations, schedules, or other concerns that make it advantageous to let each team manage their sites independently. Sites in the same realm can share the product catalog for product data. Sites in different realms can’t share data through the catalog structure.\nL14: However, import data into different realms to share it. Example: Let’s say that you have sites with separate brands in Europe and the Pacific Rim. Optionally, configure a realm for the team managing the Pacific Rim sites and another realm for Europe.\nL15: The primary instance group has three instances, which merchants use to add data, test code, and run live sites. A realm can have only one PIG per realm.\nL16: \nL17: The secondary instance group (SIG) contains your sandbox instances. The minimum number of sandboxes in a SIG is 3, and the maximum is 47.\nL18: \nL19: A B2C Commerce instance contains the tools and resources for customizing storefronts. Access an instance through your browser, or use Business Manager.\nL20: There are four instance types: Sandbox, Staging, Development, and Production. Depending on the type, the instance is either in the realm’s primary instance group (PIG) or a secondary instance group (SIG).\nL21:  | Sandboxes  | Staging  | Development  | Production\nL22: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL23: Usage  | Used by customer developers to create and update storefront code. Used by customer developers to create and update storefront code.  | Merchandising work is done here.  | Simulates the production environment. Used as the final step in testing the intersection of content and code. Goes through the CDN provided by B2C Commerce, but doesn’t cache.  | Live instance used for storefront transactions. Connected to the CDN provided by B2C Commerce. If active merchandising is enabled, collects active data.\nL24: Storefront toolkit is disabled.\nL25: Location  | SIG  | PIG  | PIG  | PIG\nL26: Data I/O  | Most system jobs are disabled for sandboxes.  | Data and code are uploaded to Staging and then replicated to Production or Development.  | Data and code are replicated from Staging. Data can be exported from the instance.  | Data and code are replicated from Staging. Active data is gathered from Production and imported into other instance types. Data can be exported from the instance and imported into Staging.\nL27: Global Release  | Updated for: Preview and General Availability  | Updated for: General Availability  | Updated for: General Availability  | Updated for: General Availability\nL28: Caching  | Caching is disabled. Full file system checks are performed.  | Caching is disabled. Full file system checks are performed.  | Caching is disabled. No file system checks are performed.  | Caching is enabled all the way through to the browser.\nL29: Depending on the size of your team, one person can play more than one role.\nL30: Role  | Instance Type  | Responsibilities\nL31: --- | --- | ---\nL32: Developer  | Sandbox, Staging  | A developer creates or modifies templates, pipelines, and scripts on a local machine and uploads them to a Sandbox instance to test. Ultimately, the developer is responsible for uploading code to the Staging instance. The developer can also export data added by merchandisers on the Staging instance to use as test data for the sandboxes. Developers never use the Development instance unless they are involved in the testing process for the production site.\nL33: Merchandiser  | Staging  | A merchandiser or online marketer is typically responsible for creating campaigns and promotions, managing product information, and configuring search behavior.\nL34: Administrator  | All instances  | An admin is responsible for granting access to instances and functionality on instances. The admin restarts instances, manages data feeds, and uploads certificates.\nL35: Quality Assurance Engineer  | Development  | This role is responsible for testing the site in conditions as close to production as possible. No code development is done on the Development instance.\nL36: If you have the correct permissions, access the B2C Commerce tools.\nL37: \nL38:   * Business Manager–Configure storefronts in real time. Categorize, price, search, display, navigate, and promote merchandise.\nL39:   * Control Center–Gives your IT staff operational control over your site, such as starting instances, and managing users.\nL40:   * Account Manager–Lets your IT staff create user accounts on B2C Commerce, grant and revoke user access to B2C Commerce instan\nL41: Import to and export from staging, development, and production instances and replicate between instances. If your storefront uses a third-party application, you are responsible for managing feeds into the catalog. Create feeds from Salesforce B2C Commerce for other backend systems, such as an order management system (OMS), enterprise resource planning system (ERP), product information management system (PIM), or warehouse management system (WMS).\nL42: B2C Commerce Feature  | Description\nL43: --- | ---\nL44: Import and Export  | Feeds data from external systems into B2C Commerce, such as product, pricing, and inventory information. You also Import and Export to move data from one instance type to another. Create XML feed files that conform to B2C Commerce schemas to import data into B2C Commerce. We recommend using the scheduled job feature to automate data import and export. Developers, administrators, and merchants must work together to determine the frequency and schedule of import feeds.\nL45: Developers are responsible for implementing the cartridges. Admins are responsible for creating the XML files containing data from the systems of record for products, pricing, and other data. They are also responsible for configuring the jobs required to run the import. Data is validated during import. If the import experiences too many errors, it can fail. To recover from a failed import, you must successfully complete another import.\nL46: Data Replication and Code Replication  | Securely moves data and code between instances. Replication is a one-way process from Staging to either a Development or Production instances. Roll back from a replication to the previous version of the instance.\nL47: Code Upload  | Used to upload code from a developer’s local machine via UX Studio to a Sandbox or Staging instance. Developers are responsible for code upload.\nL48: Catalog Feeds  | Used to process .zip files. This feature is only for additional information that is added to the catalog that isn’t part of the normal catalog import and export process.\nL49: The Batch Processing feature isn’t used for data input and output.\nL50: \nL51: In B2C Commerce, a site is the application and associated code that runs a storefront. A storefront the user’s online experience. A site can have multiple storefronts with different URLs for different brands, locales (with currency and tax differences), or multiple channels. If you are referring to a specific URL, B2C Commerce uses the term storefront.\nL52: \nL53: When designing your site, your architect must consider the following:\nL54:   * The geographical relationship between storefronts and the teams that maintain them\nL55:   * Data that is shared between sites, such as product data, tax and payment information, user roles and permissions, promotions, or application code\nL56:   * Whether the storefront must be localized for different market locales or restructured for them\nL57:   * Data that must remain separately controlled due to corporate structure or legal requirements\nL58:   * Whether customer, basket, or transaction data carries over from one storefront to the next Example: An apparel retailer lets customers shop simultaneously at both their adult and children’s sites. But a retailer with two different customer bases, such as a book publisher for religious books and fantasy fiction, can prevent crossover.\nL59: The simplest scenario is one site and one storefront that it supports.\nL60: \nL61: If you have many storefronts and a single team maintaining them, manage them more easily as a single site, even if the products are different for each site. Similarly, if you want to share baskets between storefronts, the storefronts must be part of a single site. If you are deploying many localized sites with similar branding and products, it’s faster and easier to manage new storefronts as an extension of an existing site.\nL62: Multiple sites each support multiple storefronts. Share site data, such as customer data, between sites.\nL63: \nL64: A live site is defined as a listing in the Manage Sites screen on the merchant’s production instance of Business Manager with a status set to Online, not including those sites with other statuses or sites that have been deleted.\nL65: \nL66:   * cite2†Build Your Site Structure†help.salesforce.com L67:   * cite1†Get Started with B2C Commerce --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://docs.stripe.com/mcp)\nciteturn6view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/markdown; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.stripe.com/mcp\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 348\nL0: # Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nL1: \nL2: Let your AI agents interact with the Stripe API by using our MCP server.\nL3: \nL4: Read this page in your terminal: [install the Stripe CLI](https://docs.stripe.com/cli/install) (v1.43.3+) and run `stripe docs`. [Reference](https://docs.stripe.com/cli/docs)\nL5: ## Start here: Integrate with Stripe using skills and plugins\nL6: \nL7: Stripe provides skills and plugins for agents to build faster and more accurately. See [Agent skills](https://docs.stripe.com/skills.md) for more information.\nL8: \nL9: **Quickstart**: Install the Stripe CLI with `npm install -g @stripe/cli` and run `stripe agent setup`.\nL10: The Stripe Model Context Protocol (MCP) server provides tools that AI agents can use to interact with the Stripe API and search Stripe’s knowledge base, including documentation and support articles.\nL11: ## Set up Stripe MCP with a plugin  (Recommended)\nL12: \nL13: [Agent plugins](https://docs.stripe.com/agents/plugin.md) for Stripe automatically configure the Stripe MCP server, install our [skills](https://docs.stripe.com/skills.md), and make sure they’re up-to-date. You get our latest capabilities without configuration or maintenance.\nL14: \nL15: To install, run:\nL16: \nL17: ```bash\nL18: npm install -g @stripe/cli@latest\nL19: stripe agent setup\nL20: ```\nL21: \nL22: This command automatically detects which agents you use, and runs the applicable commands.\nL23: ## Manually set up Stripe MCP\nL24: \nL25: If your MCP client doesn’t support [agent plugins](https://docs.stripe.com/agents/plugin.md) for Stripe, or you prefer to configure the Stripe MCP server and install the skills manually, follow these instructions:\nL26: #### Cursor\nL27: \nL28: [Install in Cursor](cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/mcp/install?name=stripe&config=eyJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL21jcC5zdHJpcGUuY29tIn0%3D)\nL29: \nL30: Select **Install in Cursor** to open Cursor and add the Stripe MCP server. To configure it manually, add the following configuration to `~/.cursor/mcp.json`.\nL31: \nL32: For more information, see [Cursor’s MCP documentation](https://docs.cursor.com/context/model-context-protocol).\nL33: ```json\nL34: {\nL35:  \"mcpServers\": {\nL36:    \"stripe\": {\nL37:      \"url\": \"https://mcp.stripe.com\"\nL38:    }\nL39:  }\nL40: }\nL41: ```\nL42: #### Claude Code\nL43: \nL44: Run the following command to add the Stripe MCP server to Claude Code:\nL45: \nL46: ```bash\nL47: claude mcp add --transport http stripe https://mcp.stripe.com/\nL48: ```\nL49: \nL50: After you add the server, authenticate Stripe with OAuth.\nL51: \nL52: ```bash\nL53: claude /mcp\nL54: ```\nL55: \nL56: To authenticate with a restricted API key instead of OAuth, use the `--header` option:\nL57: \nL58: ```bash\nL59: claude mcp add --transport http stripe https://mcp.stripe.com/ --header 'Authorization: Bearer <<YOUR_SECRET_KEY>>'\nL60: ```\nL61: For more information, see [Claude Code MCP documentation](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/mcp#configure-mcp-servers).\nL62: #### ChatGPT and Codex\nL63: \nL64: ### ChatGPT\nL65: \nL66: With a ChatGPT Plus, Pro, Business, Enterprise, or Education account, you can [install the Stripe plugin in ChatGPT](https://chatgpt.com/plugins/plugin_connector_690ab09fa43c8191bca40280e4563238).\nL67: ### Codex CLI\nL68: \nL69: To use the Stripe MCP server with the Codex CLI, add it to your `~/.codex/config.toml` file. To learn more, see the OpenAI [Codex MCP documentation](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/extend/mcp).\nL70: \nL71: ```toml\nL72: [mcp_servers.stripe]\nL73: url = \"https://mcp.stripe.com\"\nL74: ```\nL75: \nL76: After you add the server, authenticate Stripe with OAuth.\nL77: \nL78: ```bash\nL79: codex mcp login stripe\nL80: ```\nL81: To authenticate with a restricted API key instead of OAuth, set `bearer_token_env_var` to the name of an environment variable that holds your key. In this example, `STRIPE_API_KEY` is a placeholder, replace it with the actual name of your environment variable.\nL82: \nL83: ```toml\nL84: [mcp_servers.stripe]\nL85: url = \"https://mcp.stripe.com\"\nL86: bearer_token_env_var = \"STRIPE_API_KEY\" # Replace with the name of your environment variable\nL87: ```\nL88: #### Other\nL89: ### Install in VS Code\nL90: \nL91: [Install in VS Code](https://vscode.dev/redirect/mcp/install?name=stripe&config=%7B%22type%22%3A%22http%22%2C%22url%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.stripe.com%22%7D)\nL92: \nL93: Select **Install in VS Code** to open VS Code and add the Stripe MCP server. To configure it manually, add the following configuration to `.vscode/mcp.json` in your workspace.\nL94: \nL95: See the [VS Code MCP documentation](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/chat/mcp-servers) for more information.\nL96: ```json\nL97: {\nL98:  \"servers\": {\nL99:    \"stripe\": {\nL100:      \"type\": \"http\",\nL101:      \"url\": \"https://mcp.stripe.com\"\nL102:    }\nL103:  }\nL104: }\nL105: ```\nL106: ### Install from another MCP client\nL107: \nL108: MCP is an open protocol supported by many clients. Follow your client’s MCP setup instructions and use `https://mcp.stripe.com` as the server URL.\nL109: \nL110: Use OAuth when your client supports it. If your client doesn’t support OAuth, use a [restricted API key](https://docs.stripe.com/keys/restricted-api-keys.md) as a bearer token in the `Authorization` header. For example, if your client accepts a `headers` property, use the following configuration:\nL111: ```json\nL112: {\nL113:  \"stripe\": {\nL114:    \"url\": \"https://mcp.stripe.com\",\nL115:    \"headers\": {\nL116:      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <restricted_api_key>\"\nL117:    }\nL118:  }\nL119: }\nL120: ```\nL121: ## Manage MCP access and sessions\nL122: \nL123: Stripe’s MCP server uses OAuth to authorize clients according to the [MCP specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-03-26/basic/authorization#2-1-1-oauth-grant-types). OAuth lets you grant and revoke access without sharing an API key with the client.\nL124: When you connect an MCP client, it opens Stripe’s OAuth consent page. Use this page to authorize the client to access your Stripe data. After you authorize an MCP client with OAuth, you can manage its session in the Stripe Dashboard.\nL125: \nL126: If your organization manages your device or network, your IT administrator might block connections to external MCP servers. When the Stripe MCP server doesn’t connect in a managed environment, ask your IT department to allow `mcp.stripe.com`.\nL127: ### Manage your sessions\nL128: \nL129: Your authorized MCP clients appear under **OAuth sessions** in your [user settings](https://dashboard.stripe.com/settings/user). To revoke a session:\nL130: \nL131: 1. Find the client in the **OAuth sessions** list.\nL132: 2. Click the overflow menu (⋯).\nL133: 3. Select **Revoke access**.\nL134: ### Manage sessions as an administrator\nL135: \nL136: To view or revoke another user’s sessions, your role must include permissions to manage team members and OAuth sessions. The **Administrator** role includes these permissions.\nL137: 1. Open [Team and security](https://dashboard.stripe.com/settings/team) in the Dashboard.\nL138: 2. Select the team member you want to manage.\nL139: 3. Find the **OAuth sessions** table.\nL140: 4. To revoke one session, click its overflow menu and select **Revoke access**. To revoke every listed session, select **Revoke all**.\nL141: Session management applies only to the account or organization and environment—live mode or a sandbox—that you’re currently viewing. Revoking a session removes access only within that scope. The session remains active for accounts outside your current scope.\nL142: \nL143: When you view an organization, the **Accounts** column shows the accounts associated with each session.\nL144: ### Manage MCP access\nL145: \nL146: Administrators can [manage MCP access](https://dashboard.stripe.com/settings/mcp-and-cli-access) in the Dashboard. Configure access separately for live mode and sandbox environments.\nL147: ## Tools\nL148: \nL149: The server exposes the following [MCP tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/tools).\nL150: \nL151: Enable human confirmation of tools and exercise caution when using the Stripe MCP with other servers to avoid prompt injection attacks. If you have feedback or want to see more tools, email us at [mcp@stripe.com](mailto:mcp@stripe.com).\nL152: | Resource | Tool | Description |\nL153: | --- | --- | --- |\nL154: | **API tools** | `stripe_api_search` | Search for Stripe API methods by keyword |\nL155: | `stripe_api_details` | Get detailed parameter information for a specific Stripe API method. |\nL156: | `stripe_api_read` | Read data with any Stripe API `GET` method |\nL157: | `stripe_api_write` | Write data with any Stripe API `POST`, `PATCH`, `PUT` and `DELETE` method |\nL158: | **Account** | `get_stripe_account_info` | [Retrieve account](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts/retrieve.md) |\nL159: | **[Analytics](https://docs.stripe.com/data/analyze-with-ai.md)** | `stripe_analytics` | - (Preview) Query metrics, analyze subscriptions and billing, and run financial reports\nL160: - (Requires Sigma) Build SQL queries based on natural language |\nL161: | **Treasury** (Public preview) | `get_balance_summary` | Displays an interactive balance summary across the Stripe balance and Treasury accounts |\nL162: | **Others** | `search_stripe_documentation` | Search the Stripe documentation for the given question and language |\nL163: | `stripe_implementation_planner` | Guides the user through Stripe products to help users accept payments, sell products online, set up billing, or build any Stripe integration |\nL164: | `send_stripe_mcp_feedback` | Submit feedback from user or agent about Stripe MCP server tools |\nL165: The Stripe MCP server exposes multiple APIs that you can call with the `stripe_api_read` and `stripe_api_write` tools. This access makes much of the API available through MCP without increasing the context window unnecessarily.\nL166: ### Supported API methods\nL167: - [List all customers](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customers/list.md)\nL168: - [Retrieve a customer](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customers/retrieve.md)\nL169: - [Create a customer](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customers/create.md)\nL170: - [Update a customer](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customers/update.md)\nL171: - [List all charges](https://docs.stripe.com/api/charges/list.md)\nL172: - [Retrieve a charge](https://docs.stripe.com/api/charges/retrieve.md)\nL173: - [List all refunds for a charge](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/list.md)\nL174: - [List all refunds](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/list.md)\nL175: - [Retrieve a refund](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/retrieve.md)\nL176: - [Create refund](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/create.md)\nL177: - [List all PaymentIntents](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_intents/list.md)\nL178: - [Retrieve a PaymentIntent](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_intents/retrieve.md)\nL179: - [List PaymentMethods](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_methods/list.md)\nL180: - [Retrieve a PaymentMethod](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_methods/retrieve.md)\nL181: - [List a customer’s PaymentMethods](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_methods/list.md)\nL182: - [Retrieve a customer’s PaymentMethod](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_methods/retrieve.md)\nL183: - [List payment method configurations](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_method_configurations/list.md)\nL184: - [Retrieve a payment method configuration](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_method_configurations/retrieve.md)\nL185: - [List payment method domains](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_method_domains/list.md)\nL186: - [Create a payment method domain](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_method_domains/create.md)\nL187: - [List all SetupIntents](https://docs.stripe.com/api/setup_intents/list.md)\nL188: - [Retrieve a SetupIntent](https://docs.stripe.com/api/setup_intents/retrieve.md)\nL189: - [List all Checkout Sessions](https://docs.stripe.com/api/checkout/sessions/list.md)\nL190: - [Retrieve a Checkout Session](https://docs.stripe.com/api/checkout/sessions/retrieve.md)\nL191: - [Retrieve a Checkout Session’s line items](https://docs.stripe.com/api/checkout/sessions/line_items.md)\nL192: - [Create a Checkout Session](https://docs.stripe.com/api/checkout/sessions/create.md)\nL193: - [List all invoices](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/list.md)\nL194: - [Retrieve an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/retrieve.md)\nL195: - [Retrieve an invoice’s line items](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoice-line-item/retrieve.md)\nL196: - [Create an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/create.md)\nL197: - [Update an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/update.md)\nL198: - [Finalize an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/finalize.md)\nL199: - [Mark an invoice as uncollectible](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/mark_uncollectible.md)\nL200: - [Void an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/void.md)\nL201: - [List all invoice items](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoiceitems/list.md)\nL202: - [Retrieve an invoice item](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoiceitems/retrieve.md)\nL203: - [Create an invoice item](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoiceitems/create.md)\nL204: - [Update an invoice item](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoiceitems/update.md)\nL205: - [List all payments for an invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoice-payment/list.md)\nL206: - [Create a preview invoice](https://docs.stripe.com/api/invoices/create_preview.md)\nL207: - [List all credit notes](https://docs.stripe.com/api/credit_notes/list.md)\nL208: - [List subscriptions](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscriptions/list.md)\nL209: - [Retrieve a subscription](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscriptions/retrieve.md)\nL210: - [Create a subscription](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscriptions/create.md)\nL211: - [Update a subscription](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscriptions/update.md)\nL212: - [Cancel a subscription](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscriptions/cancel.md)\nL213: - [List all schedules](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscription_schedules/list.md)\nL214: - [Retrieve a schedule](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscription_schedules/retrieve.md)\nL215: - [List all subscription items](https://docs.stripe.com/api/subscription_items/list.md)\nL216: - [List all coupons](https://docs.stripe.com/api/coupons/list.md)\nL217: - [Retrieve a coupon](https://docs.stripe.com/api/coupons/retrieve.md)\nL218: - [Create a coupon](https://docs.stripe.com/api/coupons/create.md)\nL219: - [Update a coupon](https://docs.stripe.com/api/coupons/update.md)\nL220: - [Delete a coupon](https://docs.stripe.com/api/coupons/delete.md)\nL221: - [List all promotion codes](https://docs.stripe.com/api/promotion_codes/list.md)\nL222: - [Retrieve a promotion code](https://docs.stripe.com/api/promotion_codes/retrieve.md)\nL223: - [Create a promotion code](https://docs.stripe.com/api/promotion_codes/create.md)\nL224: - [List all products](https://docs.stripe.com/api/products/list.md)\nL225: - [Retrieve a product](https://docs.stripe.com/api/products/retrieve.md)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMCP server – Linear Docs (https://linear.app/docs/mcp)\nciteturn6view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://linear.app/docs/mcp\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 402\n"
